@prefix gdprkg:  <https://w3id.org/gdprkg#> .
@prefix ex:      <https://w3id.org/gdprkg/data#> .
@prefix eli:     <http://data.europa.eu/eli/ontology#> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix oa:      <http://www.w3.org/ns/oa#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#################################################################
#  Article 46 — RDF instantiation (ABox)  [PHASE 5]
#  ---------------------------------------------------------------
#  Status: 0.1.0-phase5-art46-draft (2026-08-07) — PROVISIONAL,
#  awaiting orchestrator verification + Zied's certification.
#  reviewStatus="draft" on every primary normative unit AND on all
#  eight TransferMechanism nodes (see the provenance note in §6).
#  NOT a validated benchmark.
#
#  Built against ontology/gdpr_kg.ttl (TBox, post the two 2026-08-07
#  Art-46 mints: gdprkg:TransferMechanism + gdprkg:providesSafeguard)
#  + validated against ontology/shapes.ttl (NOT edited). Executed per
#  the signed-off brief v2 (../../gdpr-kg-monitoring/
#  art46_instantiation_brief.md) §3.6 FIXED inventory + work order E.
#  Source text: kg/source/gdpr_articles_structured.json article "46",
#  13 rows (5 paragraph + 8 point); §0 structure gate PASSED 5/5
#  (2026-08-05). Encoding verified: 4 x U+2019, 0 x U+FFFD.
#
#  >>> THE CORE FRAMING (read before editing): Article 46 is a
#  TRANSFER-GATE article, not a disclosure article and not a
#  lawful-basis article. 46(1) is a PERMISSION bounded by two
#  necessary Conditions and switched off entirely by an Art 45(3)
#  adequacy decision. 46(2)/(3) enumerate INSTRUMENTS, not duties:
#  the stems read "the appropriate safeguards ... may be provided
#  for ... BY:", and that "by" is INSTRUMENTAL. Those eight nodes
#  are gdprkg:TransferMechanism — NOT Obligations, NOT a Safeguard
#  subclass, and NOT gdprkg:LegalBasis (that typing is forbidden:
#  it would fire carry f2-domain-family, and DPV-GDPR's own
#  eu-gdpr:A46-2-a...f take exactly that typing, which is why this
#  pass declines to align to them — see gdpr_kg.ttl on
#  gdprkg:TransferMechanism).
#
#  >>> FIRST-IN-PROJECT events in this file, all deliberate:
#    * FIRST ABox use of gdprkg:notApplicableIf ANYWHERE (1 edge,
#      on the 46(1) Permission -> the 45(3) adequacy Condition).
#      Domain-clean: notApplicableIf's domain is NormativeElement
#      and Permission is a NormativeElement, so F2 is NOT tripped.
#    * FIRST non-controller Obligation bearer (46(4) ->
#      ex:actor-supervisory-authority). All 58 pre-existing
#      imposesObligationOn triples target ex:actor-controller.
#    * FIRST use of gdprkg:TransferMechanism / providesSafeguard.
#    * FIRST instance mint of ex:actor-processor (gdprkg:Processor
#      was declared in Phase 3 and idle since).
#
#  >>> DELIBERATELY ABSENT (assert these as expected ZERO):
#    * NO gdprkg:Exception  — Art 46 states no exemption grounds.
#    * NO gdprkg:LegalEffect and NO hasLegalEffect — T3-D declined
#      LegalEffect's first use for 46(5); it deserves a clean
#      paradigm case (Art 7(2) "not binding").
#    * NO disclosesConcept / disclosedToDataSubject — not a
#      disclosure article (the Art-12 12G posture).
#    * NO gdprkg:LegalBasis of any kind.
#    * NO normative unit for 46(5) — transitional law, out of scope.
#
#  >>> ITEMS SURFACED FOR REVIEW (do NOT treat as settled) are in
#      articles/art46_surfaced_items.md (EXEC-1...EXEC-12). Key ones
#      are flagged inline with  #!SURFACE .
#################################################################

<https://w3id.org/gdprkg/data/art46>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "GDPR Article 46 instance graph (ABox)"@en ;
    owl:versionInfo "0.1.0-phase5-art46-draft (2026-08-07) — provisional, awaiting orchestrator verification + Zied's certification. 1 Permission + 1 Obligation + 8 TransferMechanism + 4 Conditions + 0 Exceptions (inventory FIXED by brief v2 §3.6). First ABox use of gdprkg:notApplicableIf anywhere; first non-controller obligation bearer; first use of gdprkg:TransferMechanism / gdprkg:providesSafeguard; ex:actor-processor minted. 5 new ReferencedProvision stubs (ref-art45-3, -art93-2, -art40, -art42, -art63); ex:ref-art47 REUSED from art13.ttl." ;
    rdfs:comment "Pilot instantiation of GDPR Article 46 against the gdprkg TBox. The project's first applicability-GATING unit: 46(1) is a Permission whose availability is switched off by an Art 45(3) adequacy decision (gdprkg:notApplicableIf, first ABox use anywhere) and conditioned on two necessary Conditions. 46(2)(a)-(f) and 46(3)(a)-(b) are eight gdprkg:TransferMechanism nodes, one per lettered Point, each providesSafeguard the canonical ex:concept-safeguard. 46(4) is one Obligation borne by the supervisory authority — the first non-controller bearer in the project. 46(5) is transitional law and yields no normative unit. reviewStatus=draft throughout."@en ;
    owl:imports <https://w3id.org/gdprkg> .

#################################################################
#  1 — LEGAL-SOURCE CHAIN  (Regulation -> Article -> Paragraph -> Point)
#  ex:gdpr is re-declared (same canonical node as art13/art6/art12/art14)
#  so this file is self-contained standalone; under the combined load
#  ex:gdpr accrues hasArticle art13, art6, art12, art14 AND art46.
#
#  ELI seeAlso: the ARTICLE-LEVEL form is used, exactly as art6.ttl:85 /
#  art12.ttl:67 / art14.ttl do. #!SURFACE-EXEC-9: this pass VERIFIED
#  (2026-08-07) that EUR-Lex returns HTTP 200 for arbitrary, non-existent
#  ELI subdivision paths (art_500, par_77, pnt_z all 200, serving the whole
#  document), so an ELI IRI's resolvability is NOT evidence that the
#  subdivision it names exists. No sub-article ELI IRI is asserted anywhere
#  in this file. The existing article-level pattern is followed for
#  consistency with four certified files, NOT because it was verifiable.
#################################################################

ex:gdpr a gdprkg:Regulation ;
    rdfs:label "Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR)"@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj> ;
    gdprkg:hasArticle ex:art46 .

ex:art46 a gdprkg:Article ;
    rdfs:label "Article 46"@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/art_46> ;
    gdprkg:workingTextSource "https://gdpr-info.eu/art-46-gdpr/"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    gdprkg:hasParagraph ex:art46-p1 , ex:art46-p2 , ex:art46-p3 , ex:art46-p4 , ex:art46-p5 .

# --- Paragraph 1 (unlettered) — the gate + the permission ---
ex:art46-p1 a gdprkg:Paragraph ; rdfs:label "Article 46(1)"@en ;
    oa:start 0 ;
    oa:end   352 .

# --- Paragraph 2 (chapeau) + six lettered points: NO supervisory-authority authorisation ---
# NB the 46(2) list is NOT qualified by "in particular" (verified on disk) — see 46(3).
ex:art46-p2 a gdprkg:Paragraph ; rdfs:label "Article 46(2)"@en ;
    oa:start 353 ;
    oa:end   1451 ;
    gdprkg:hasPoint ex:art46-p2-a , ex:art46-p2-b , ex:art46-p2-c ,
                    ex:art46-p2-d , ex:art46-p2-e , ex:art46-p2-f .

ex:art46-p2-a a gdprkg:Point ; rdfs:label "Article 46(2)(a)"@en ;
    oa:start 510 ;
    oa:end   596 .
ex:art46-p2-b a gdprkg:Point ; rdfs:label "Article 46(2)(b)"@en ;
    oa:start 597 ;
    oa:end   655 .
ex:art46-p2-c a gdprkg:Point ; rdfs:label "Article 46(2)(c)"@en ;
    oa:start 656 ;
    oa:end   793 .
ex:art46-p2-d a gdprkg:Point ; rdfs:label "Article 46(2)(d)"@en ;
    oa:start 794 ;
    oa:end   964 .
ex:art46-p2-e a gdprkg:Point ; rdfs:label "Article 46(2)(e)"@en ;
    oa:start 965 ;
    oa:end   1205 .
ex:art46-p2-f a gdprkg:Point ; rdfs:label "Article 46(2)(f)"@en ;
    oa:start 1206 ;
    oa:end   1451 .

# --- Paragraph 3 (chapeau) + two lettered points: authorisation REQUIRED ---
# !! The 46(3) list IS qualified by "in particular" — it is NON-EXHAUSTIVE, and 46(2) is
# not. This asymmetry is the single most consequential structural fact in the article for
# the applicability claim: a closed-world inference ("no listed instrument matched =>
# no appropriate safeguard") is defensible over 46(2) and UNSOUND over 46(3). It binds
# fixtures/art46_scenarios.ttl and every query written against this file.
ex:art46-p3 a gdprkg:Paragraph ; rdfs:label "Article 46(3)"@en ;
    oa:start 1452 ;
    oa:end   1968 ;
    gdprkg:hasPoint ex:art46-p3-a , ex:art46-p3-b .

ex:art46-p3-a a gdprkg:Point ; rdfs:label "Article 46(3)(a)"@en ;
    oa:start 1625 ;
    oa:end   1807 .
ex:art46-p3-b a gdprkg:Point ; rdfs:label "Article 46(3)(b)"@en ;
    oa:start 1808 ;
    oa:end   1968 .

# --- Paragraph 4 (unlettered) — the supervisory-authority duty ---
ex:art46-p4 a gdprkg:Paragraph ; rdfs:label "Article 46(4)"@en ;
    oa:start 1969 ;
    oa:end   2118 .

# --- Paragraph 5 (unlettered) — TRANSITIONAL LAW, no normative unit (T3-D) ---
# 46(5) saves two classes of pre-GDPR instrument: Member-State/supervisory-authority
# authorisations under Directive 95/46/EC Art 26(2), and Commission decisions under
# Art 26(4). "shall remain valid" / "shall remain in force" is neither a duty nor a power —
# it is a statement about an instrument's continued validity, which is what
# gdprkg:LegalEffect was reserved for. T3-D DECLINED that first use: LegalEffect deserves a
# clean paradigm case (Art 7(2) "not binding"), and 46(5) contributes nothing to the gating
# claim. Recorded per the Art-12 12(8) precedent — but see the next note.
#
# #!SURFACE-EXEC-2 — THE 12(8) PRECEDENT COULD NOT BE FOLLOWED IN FULL, and this is a
# reported gap, not an oversight. Art-12's 12(8) records its out-of-scope reference as
# `ex:art12-p8 rdfs:seeAlso ex:ref-art92` — a seeAlso pointing at a MINTED STUB. Here T3-E
# authorises exactly FIVE stubs and NO Directive 95/46/EC stub, so there is no node to point
# at; and gdprkg:referencesProvision cannot originate on a Paragraph (its domain is
# NormativeElement, so a Paragraph subject would be entailed a GroundedConcept and BREAK
# CQ-C2 — the exact reason 12(8) used seeAlso in the first place). An external ELI IRI for
# Directive 95/46/EC Art 26 was investigated as the seeAlso target and REJECTED on evidence:
# EUR-Lex returns HTTP 200 for fabricated subdivisions (dir/1995/46/art_999/oj and
# art_26/par_77/oj both 200), so no such IRI is verifiable. RULED by Zied 2026-08-07:
# skos:note text only, recorded as a non-coverage under playbook §4.1. Tier-1 "new stubs = 5"
# is unchanged. Ledger carry: art46-46-5-seealso-target.
ex:art46-p5 a gdprkg:Paragraph ; rdfs:label "Article 46(5)"@en ;
    oa:start 2119 ;
    oa:end   2574 ;
    skos:scopeNote "OUT-OF-SCOPE TRANSITIONAL LAW (T3-D). Cites Directive 95/46/EC Art 26(2) (first sentence) and Art 26(4) (second sentence) — the project's first citations to an instrument outside the GDPR entirely. NO ReferencedProvision stub is minted for either (T3-E), NO rdfs:seeAlso target is asserted (no verifiable external IRI exists — see the block comment above), and NO normative unit is generated. Recorded as text only. This is a deliberate, ruled non-coverage, not an omission."@en .

#################################################################
#  1b — REFERENCED PROVISIONS — FIVE NEW STUBS (T3-E), never populated
#
#  REUSE-FIRST, grep-verified 2026-08-07 across every articles/*.ttl before
#  minting: ex:ref-art47 ALREADY EXISTS (art13.ttl:124) and is REUSED, NOT
#  re-declared here — it appears below only as an edge target. Seventeen live
#  gdprkg:ReferencedProvision individuals existed before this pass; this file
#  adds five, taking the total to twenty-two.
#
#  #!SURFACE-EXEC-3 — THE STUBS STAY BARE (type + label), with NO external
#  anchor, and that is a RULED outcome rather than an unexamined default.
#  Reuse gate R-3 (2026-08-07) established that GDPRtEXT — the methodological
#  anchor for R4 point-level grounding [pandit2018gdprtext] — is ENTIRELY
#  UNREACHABLE: https://w3id.org/GDPRtEXT returns 503, as does the ADAPT
#  Centre host root; http://purl.org/adaptcentre/... returns 404. A 2020
#  GitHub mirror DOES contain exactly the five anchors needed
#  (gdpr:article45-3, article93-2, article40, article42, article63), each
#  typed eli:LegalResourceSubdivision — the very class
#  gdprkg:ReferencedProvision is rdfs:subClassOf — so the alignment would
#  have been type-clean. Zied RULED (2026-08-07) that a link known to 404
#  inside a file about to be certified is worse than no link. Recorded as a
#  non-coverage in cq_sparql/expected_results.md. Ledger carry:
#  gdprtext-vocabulary-unreachable.
#################################################################

ex:ref-art45-3 a gdprkg:ReferencedProvision ;
    rdfs:label "Article 45(3) (adequacy decision; referenced, out of scope)"@en .
    # POINT-LEVEL stub, following the ex:ref-art9-2-a precedent (art13.ttl:121): the gate in
    # 46(1) is specifically 45(3), not Article 45 as a whole.
ex:ref-art93-2 a gdprkg:ReferencedProvision ;
    rdfs:label "Article 93(2) (examination procedure; referenced, out of scope)"@en .
    # ONE stub, cited TWICE (46(2)(c) and 46(2)(d)) — per-provision, not per-citation.
ex:ref-art40 a gdprkg:ReferencedProvision ;
    rdfs:label "Article 40 (codes of conduct; referenced, out of scope)"@en .
ex:ref-art42 a gdprkg:ReferencedProvision ;
    rdfs:label "Article 42 (certification; referenced, out of scope)"@en .
ex:ref-art63 a gdprkg:ReferencedProvision ;
    rdfs:label "Article 63 (consistency mechanism; referenced, out of scope)"@en .

# ex:ref-art47 — REUSED from art13.ttl (Article 47, binding corporate rules), cited by
#   46(2)(b). NOT re-declared here. Note art13.ttl and art14.ttl both reference it on the
#   same three-way referencesProvision line the ex:ref-art46 rewire touches; that line keeps
#   ref-art47 and ref-art49 intact.
# NO Directive 95/46/EC stub — T3-E, follows from T3-D. See the 46(5) block above.

#################################################################
#  2 — PROVENANCE (one shared record for the single Art-46 mapping pass)
#  Parallel to art13/art6/art12/art14. ex:agent-mapper re-declared (same
#  shared canonical agent).
#################################################################

ex:prov-art46 a gdprkg:ProvenanceRecord ; rdfs:label "Provenance: Art-46 initial mapping (draft)"@en ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy ex:act-art46-mapping ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo ex:agent-mapper .
ex:act-art46-mapping a gdprkg:MappingReviewActivity ; rdfs:label "Art-46 extraction/mapping activity"@en ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith ex:agent-mapper .
ex:agent-mapper a gdprkg:Reviewer ; rdfs:label "Mapping agent (pending human/legal sign-off)"@en .

#################################################################
#  3 — ACTORS
#  REUSED (type re-declared only, no duplicate identity — the art14 pattern):
#    ex:actor-controller, ex:actor-supervisory-authority.
#  MINTED: ex:actor-processor (T3-I) — an INSTANCE mint, NOT a TBox change:
#    gdprkg:Processor has been in the TBox since Phase 3 (gdpr_kg.ttl:228,
#    owl:equivalentClass dpv:DataProcessor) and has been idle until now.
#
#  #!SURFACE-EXEC-4 — SAME WORDS, TWO PARTIES. "A controller or processor" in
#  46(1) and 46(3)(a) is the TRANSFEROR (the party making the transfer, who
#  holds the permission). In 46(2)(e)/(f) the identical phrase reads "the
#  controller or processor IN THE THIRD COUNTRY" — the IMPORTER, who gives the
#  binding commitments. These are DIFFERENT PARTIES in the same article and
#  MUST NOT be resolved by string match. T3-I directs that NO separate
#  third-country importer actor is minted this pass; the two mechanism nodes
#  that mention the importer (mech-46-2-e, mech-46-2-f) therefore carry NO
#  actor edge at all rather than a wrong one. The distinction is recorded, not
#  modelled. Do not "fix" this by pointing them at ex:actor-processor.
#################################################################

ex:actor-controller a gdprkg:Controller ;
    rdfs:label "Controller"@en .
ex:actor-supervisory-authority a gdprkg:Actor_SupervisoryAuthority ;
    rdfs:label "Supervisory authority"@en .
ex:actor-processor a gdprkg:Processor ;
    rdfs:label "Processor"@en ;
    # S-C Task 4: this was a skos:note. It is a MODELLING JUSTIFICATION,
    # not legal text, and ex:actor-processor has no source unit to be an
    # anchor for, so the skos:note triple is deleted rather than split.
    # REPAIR-PLAN.md 6.5 / "S-C Task 4".
    rdfs:comment "Minted by the Art-46 pass (T3-I). 46(1) permits 'a controller OR processor' to transfer — disjunctive in addressee, both permitted — so the 46(1) Permission carries TWO grantsPermissionTo edges. gdprkg:Processor was declared in Phase 3 and idle until now; this is an instance mint, not a TBox change."@en .

#################################################################
#  4 — CANONICAL DOMAIN NODES (REUSED, type re-declared only)
#
#  ex:concept-safeguard is the target of all eight providesSafeguard edges.
#
#  #!SURFACE-EXEC-5 — THE CANONICAL-SHARED-NODE TEST, and its honest result.
#  Work order E §4.2 names Art 46 as "the cleanest available test of the
#  canonical-shared-node claim", because ex:concept-transfer / -safeguard /
#  -third-country already exist and Art 46 is the article those nodes are
#  ABOUT, where Arts 13/14 merely DISCLOSED them. Result, stated either way as
#  required: Art 46 attaches to ONE of the three (ex:concept-safeguard, via
#  the new gdprkg:providesSafeguard, 8 edges). It attaches to NEITHER
#  ex:concept-transfer NOR ex:concept-third-country, because no existing
#  property runs from a Permission or a TransferMechanism to a Transfer or a
#  TransferDestination, and inventing one would be a third TBox addition
#  (work order E §3 = STOP). So the claim is CONFIRMED for the safeguard node
#  and UNTESTED for the other two — not refuted, but not demonstrated either.
#
#  #!SURFACE-EXEC-6 — the reused labels are now questionable. ex:concept-safeguard
#  is labelled "Appropriate/suitable safeguards (Art 13(1)(f))" and
#  ex:concept-transfer "Transfer to third country/international org (Art 13(1)(f))"
#  (art13.ttl:163, :160). Art 46 is the article these nodes are really about, so
#  an Art-13-specific label on a node now shared by three articles is misleading.
#  REPORTED, NOT ACTED ON: art13.ttl is certified and relabelling it is outside
#  this work order's authorized edit surface (WO-D §10 item 8).
#################################################################

ex:concept-safeguard a gdprkg:Safeguard .
    # REUSED from art13.ttl:163 (also re-declared in art14.ttl:243). NOT relabelled here.

#################################################################
#  5 — CONDITIONS (4; grounded; inherit provenance via their parent norm)
#
#  Conditions are Tier-2 — the brief fixes the Permission/Obligation/Mechanism
#  counts but leaves the Condition count to the executor (brief §3.6). Four are
#  built, which is the brief's stated minimum: 46(1)'s two, the 45(3) gate, and
#  46(4)'s "cases referred to in paragraph 3".
#
#  #!SURFACE-EXEC-7 — CONDITION (ii) STAYS ONE NODE. T3-A: "enforceable data
#  subject rights and effective legal remedies for data subjects are available"
#  is a SINGLE CONJUNCTIVE STATE OF THE WORLD, not two independently-existing
#  things — unlike 13(2)(b)'s six rights, which the R5 split separates. It is
#  NOT split. This is a legal reading, carried to the finished-work review.
#################################################################

# 46(1) condition (i) — the safeguards limb.
ex:cond-46-1-safeguards-provided a gdprkg:Condition ;
    rdfs:label "the controller or processor has provided appropriate safeguards"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p1 .

# 46(1) condition (ii) — the remedies limb. ONE node (T3-A), not two.
ex:cond-46-1-enforceable-rights-remedies a gdprkg:Condition ;
    rdfs:label "enforceable data subject rights and effective legal remedies for data subjects are available"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p1 .

# THE 45(3) GATE — attached to the Permission via gdprkg:notApplicableIf (T3-A-polarity, G2).
#
# #!SURFACE-EXEC-8 — THE POLARITY CHOICE, AND WHY THE NODE READS POSITIVELY.
# The condition asserts that an adequacy decision EXISTS; the NEGATION lives in the
# gdprkg:notApplicableIf EDGE, not in the node. Zied's recorded reason: the condition node
# stays REUSABLE (Art 45 itself, or Art 49, can attach to this same fact later) and the
# negation is not baked into it. The positive-polarity variant G1 — appliesIf -> "no
# adequacy decision exists" — was CONSIDERED AND DECLINED. Verified domain-clean:
# notApplicableIf has rdfs:domain gdprkg:NormativeElement and gdprkg:Permission is
# rdfs:subClassOf gdprkg:NormativeElement, so carry f2-domain-family is NOT tripped
# (brief v1 was wrong to imply it was). This is the property's FIRST ABox use anywhere in
# the project, on precisely the claim the NOT_APPLICABLE story rests on.
ex:cond-46-1-adequacy-decision-exists a gdprkg:Condition ;
    rdfs:label "an adequacy decision under Article 45(3) exists for the third country, territory or international organisation"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p1 ;
    gdprkg:referencesProvision ex:ref-art45-3 .

# 46(4)'s scope condition — the duty bites only on the 46(3) authorisation route.
ex:cond-46-4-authorisation-cases a gdprkg:Condition ;
    rdfs:label "the case is one referred to in paragraph 3 (a transfer requiring authorisation from the competent supervisory authority)"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p4 .

#################################################################
#  6 — THE EIGHT TRANSFER MECHANISMS (46(2)(a)-(f), 46(3)(a)-(b))
#
#  T3-B / U5: one node per lettered point, each groundedIn ITS OWN Point (R4).
#  The (c)/(d) merge was DECLINED (they differ by adoption route, which is
#  legally meaningful). The (e)/(f) split at "together with binding and
#  enforceable commitments" was DECLINED (that phrase defines ONE instrument,
#  not two).
#
#  WHY U5 AND NOT A CONDITION PER INSTRUMENT — the decisive argument was
#  EXPRESSIVENESS, not preference. Both lists are DISJUNCTIVE (one instrument
#  suffices), but gdprkg:appliesIf is CONJUNCTIVE BY CONSTRUCTION: attaching
#  eight Conditions to the 46(1) Permission would assert that ALL EIGHT must
#  hold. THE MODEL HAS NO OR. U5 dissolves the need — the disjunction lives in
#  the typing of a concrete mechanism, not in an operator. (Recorded convention,
#  previously tacit: multiple appliesIf = AND; multiple hasException = OR. An
#  AND/OR construct is DEFERRED by Zied to after Art 46, and the reuse gate
#  applies first — ODRL LogicalConstraint is the candidate to verify.)
#
#  PROVENANCE ON THESE EIGHT — ASSERTED BUT NOT ENFORCED. Ruled by Zied
#  2026-08-07. Each carries hasProvenance + reviewStatus "draft" so it is
#  traceable and visible to CQ-C6's direct branch (which keys on the TRIPLES,
#  not on class). But gdprsh:ProvenanceRequiredShape and gdprsh:ReviewStatusShape
#  both target gdprkg:PrimaryNormativeUnit, and gdprkg:TransferMechanism is
#  deliberately NOT one (that would re-import the reading T3-B rejected). So
#  NO SHAPE FIRES: a later article could mint a TransferMechanism with no
#  provenance and nothing would fail. The enforcing shape is scheduled to the
#  deferred I4/I7 SHACL batch and is NOT built here. Ledger carry:
#  art46-transfermechanism-provenance.
#
#  #!SURFACE-EXEC-1 — CITATIONS RIDE ON rdfs:seeAlso, NOT referencesProvision.
#  THE MOST CONSEQUENTIAL EXECUTOR CALL IN THIS FILE; reversible in one line.
#  gdprkg:referencesProvision has rdfs:domain gdprkg:NormativeElement. A
#  gdprkg:TransferMechanism is NOT a NormativeElement (it is a GroundedConcept
#  only), so attaching referencesProvision to one would ENTAIL it is a
#  NormativeElement — an accidental re-typing of exactly the SURFACE-F species,
#  re-importing the "these are norms" reading that T3-B/U5 rejected. It would
#  NOT break anything mechanically (NormativeElement is in neither disjoint set;
#  CQ-C1's FILTER lists only Obligation/Condition/Exception; CQ-C2 is unaffected
#  since these nodes are grounded), which is precisely why it is a smell rather
#  than an error. Following the Art-12 12(8) precedent for the same domain
#  mismatch, the five point-level citations are recorded as ANNOTATIONS
#  (rdfs:seeAlso) instead. COST, stated plainly: the Art-46 instrument citations
#  are NOT traversable via referencesProvision, so ref-art93-2 / ref-art40 /
#  ref-art42 carry ZERO referencesProvision in-edges. IF ZIED PREFERS THE OTHER
#  SIDE OF THIS TRADE, the change is a literal find-and-replace of "rdfs:seeAlso"
#  with "gdprkg:referencesProvision" on the five lines below, plus a +5 revision
#  to the referencesProvision fixture in expected_results.md. NOT DECIDED HERE.
#################################################################

ex:mech-46-2-a a gdprkg:TransferMechanism ;
    rdfs:label "A legally binding and enforceable instrument between public authorities or bodies (Art 46(2)(a))"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p2-a ;
    gdprkg:providesSafeguard ex:concept-safeguard ;
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .

ex:mech-46-2-b a gdprkg:TransferMechanism ;
    rdfs:label "Binding corporate rules in accordance with Article 47 (Art 46(2)(b))"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p2-b ;
    gdprkg:providesSafeguard ex:concept-safeguard ;
    rdfs:seeAlso ex:ref-art47 ;          # REUSED stub (art13.ttl:124) — see EXEC-1
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .

ex:mech-46-2-c a gdprkg:TransferMechanism ;
    rdfs:label "Standard data protection clauses adopted by the Commission (Art 46(2)(c))"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p2-c ;
    gdprkg:providesSafeguard ex:concept-safeguard ;
    rdfs:seeAlso ex:ref-art93-2 ;        # see EXEC-1
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .

ex:mech-46-2-d a gdprkg:TransferMechanism ;
    rdfs:label "Standard data protection clauses adopted by a supervisory authority and approved by the Commission (Art 46(2)(d))"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p2-d ;
    gdprkg:providesSafeguard ex:concept-safeguard ;
    rdfs:seeAlso ex:ref-art93-2 ;        # SAME stub as (c) — one stub, two citations
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .
    # (c) and (d) are NOT merged (T3-B declined U2): both are "standard data protection
    # clauses", but they differ by ADOPTION ROUTE — Commission vs supervisory authority —
    # which is legally meaningful and is what the two Points separately establish.

ex:mech-46-2-e a gdprkg:TransferMechanism ;
    rdfs:label "An approved code of conduct pursuant to Article 40, together with binding and enforceable commitments of the controller or processor in the third country (Art 46(2)(e))"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p2-e ;
    gdprkg:providesSafeguard ex:concept-safeguard ;
    rdfs:seeAlso ex:ref-art40 ;          # see EXEC-1
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .
    # NOT split at "together with ..." (T3-B declined U3): the commitments do not stand as a
    # separate instrument, they QUALIFY this one. The "controller or processor in the third
    # country" here is the IMPORTER, not the transferor — EXEC-4; no actor edge is drawn.

ex:mech-46-2-f a gdprkg:TransferMechanism ;
    rdfs:label "An approved certification mechanism pursuant to Article 42, together with binding and enforceable commitments of the controller or processor in the third country (Art 46(2)(f))"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p2-f ;
    gdprkg:providesSafeguard ex:concept-safeguard ;
    rdfs:seeAlso ex:ref-art42 ;          # see EXEC-1
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .
    # IMPORTER again (EXEC-4). Not split (EXEC-7 sibling reasoning).

ex:mech-46-3-a a gdprkg:TransferMechanism ;
    rdfs:label "Contractual clauses between the controller or processor and the controller, processor or recipient in the third country or international organisation (Art 46(3)(a)) — AUTHORISATION REQUIRED"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p3-a ;
    gdprkg:providesSafeguard ex:concept-safeguard ;
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .

ex:mech-46-3-b a gdprkg:TransferMechanism ;
    rdfs:label "Provisions inserted into administrative arrangements between public authorities or bodies which include enforceable and effective data subject rights (Art 46(3)(b)) — AUTHORISATION REQUIRED"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p3-b ;
    gdprkg:providesSafeguard ex:concept-safeguard ;
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .

# #!SURFACE-EXEC-10 — THE AUTHORISATION REGIME IS IN THE LABEL, NOT IN AN EDGE.
# 46(2) instruments need NO supervisory-authority authorisation; 46(3) instruments DO. That
# is the structural difference between the two paragraphs and it is real. It is recorded in
# the two 46(3) labels and in the paragraph skos:notes, NOT as a Condition, because
# gdprkg:appliesIf has rdfs:domain gdprkg:PrimaryNormativeUnit — attaching a Condition to a
# TransferMechanism would entail the mechanism IS a PrimaryNormativeUnit, which is exactly
# the typing this unit deliberately refuses. There is no domain-clean way to attach the
# regime to the mechanism with the current vocabulary. Surfaced, not solved.

#################################################################
#  7 — THE 46(1) PERMISSION (1) — the applicability gate itself
#################################################################

ex:perm-46-1-transfer a gdprkg:Permission ;
    rdfs:label "May transfer personal data to a third country or an international organisation, only if appropriate safeguards have been provided and enforceable data subject rights and effective legal remedies are available (Art 46(1))"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p1 ;
    # TWO bearer edges (T3-I): "a controller OR processor" is disjunctive in addressee and
    # BOTH are permitted. This is the first multi-bearer norm in the project.
    gdprkg:grantsPermissionTo ex:actor-controller , ex:actor-processor ;
    # The two necessary conditions. Multiple appliesIf = AND (recorded convention) — which
    # is CORRECT here: 46(1) requires safeguards AND remedies, conjunctively.
    gdprkg:appliesIf ex:cond-46-1-safeguards-provided ,
                     ex:cond-46-1-enforceable-rights-remedies ;
    # THE GATE. First ABox use of gdprkg:notApplicableIf anywhere in the project.
    gdprkg:notApplicableIf ex:cond-46-1-adequacy-decision-exists ;
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .

#################################################################
#  8 — THE 46(4) OBLIGATION (1) — first non-controller bearer in the project
#
#  Structurally free: ex:actor-supervisory-authority already existed
#  (art13.ttl:135, so far used only as a referencesActor TARGET), and
#  gdprsh:ObligationBearerShape (shapes.ttl:164-175) requires only
#  `sh:class gdprkg:Actor` without hardcoding the controller — so this conforms
#  with NO shape edit, and no shape edit is permitted.
#
#  NOTE FOR CQ-C3: this is the +1 that takes the locked count 58 -> 59, and it
#  is NOT a controller. The CQ-C3 result set stops being homogeneous for the
#  first time in the project's history.
#
#  NO gdprkg:referencesActor edge is drawn. The supervisory authority is the
#  BEARER here, and referencesActor is for the NON-BEARER actor a duty is about
#  (its whole point, per the 2026-07-03 mint). 46(4) references no third party.
#  CQ-C4 therefore takes a ZERO delta from this article — asserted explicitly in
#  expected_results.md so a later nonzero is a defect signal, not a discovery.
#################################################################

ex:obl-46-4-consistency-mechanism a gdprkg:Obligation ;
    rdfs:label "Apply the consistency mechanism referred to in Article 63 in the cases referred to in paragraph 3"@en ;
    gdprkg:groundedIn ex:art46-p4 ;
    gdprkg:imposesObligationOn ex:actor-supervisory-authority ;
    gdprkg:appliesIf ex:cond-46-4-authorisation-cases ;
    gdprkg:referencesProvision ex:ref-art63 ;   # domain-clean: an Obligation IS a NormativeElement
    gdprkg:hasProvenance ex:prov-art46 ; gdprkg:reviewStatus "draft" .

#################################################################
#  END — Article 46 ABox.
#  1 Permission (2 bearer edges) + 1 Obligation (supervisory authority)
#  + 8 TransferMechanism (8 providesSafeguard -> ex:concept-safeguard)
#  + 4 Conditions + 0 Exceptions.
#  1 notApplicableIf | 0 hasLegalEffect | 0 LegalEffect | 0 LegalBasis
#  0 disclosesConcept | 0 disclosedToDataSubject | 0 hasTimeLimit
#  5 new ReferencedProvision stubs; ex:ref-art47 reused; 1 actor minted.
#  46(5) => ZERO normative units (transitional law, T3-D).
#################################################################
